1080	\section{Applying the classical low-degree test}
1081	\label{sec:apply-ldt}
1082	
1083	The combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next
1084	merged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that
1085	encode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is
1086	done by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking
1087	the quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test
1088	(Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness}). The classical game, its question
1089	distribution, and the line-point distribution are defined at dimension $2m+2$
1090	only when $2m+2$ divides $q$ (Definition~\ref{def:ld-game}), a condition that
1091	admissibility of $(q,m,d)$ does not supply; the statements below are the
1092	source statements, without that condition, and their proofs are blocked at
1093	the instantiation step for admissible parameters with $m \ge 2$. The
1094	obstruction is recorded in Remark~\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility} and
1095	analyzed, together with candidate repairs, in
1096	\cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
